.\" trash-put(1) manual page for trash-cli.
.TH TRASH-PUT 1 2026-11-14 "trash-cli 0.26.9.29" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
trash\-put \- trash files and directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B trash\-put
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.IR FILE ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B trash\-put
moves files and directories into the trash can defined by the FreeDesktop.org
Trash Specification, recording the original location and the deletion time of
each entry so that it can be listed with
.BR trash\-list (1),
restored with
.BR trash\-restore (1),
removed selectively with
.BR trash\-rm (1),
or purged with
.BR trash\-empty (1).
.PP
All six trash\-cli commands are one program that dispatches on the name it is
invoked as;
\fBtrash\fR
is an alias of
\fBtrash\-put\fR,
and every diagnostic uses the invoked name.
.PP
Trashable entries:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
regular files, empty or not;
.IP \(bu 2
directories, whole and recursively, read\-only directories included: only the
parent directory needs write and search permission;
.IP \(bu 2
symbolic links: the link itself is trashed, never its target, dangling links
included;
.IP \(bu 2
FIFOs, sockets and device nodes.
.RE
.PP
The entry is moved with rename(2), so its permissions, timestamps and
ownership survive the round trip.
.PP
A request is refused when the basename of the argument is exactly
\fB.\fR
or
\fB..\fR,
when the path does not exist (unless
\fB\-f\fR
is in effect), when the parent directory does not allow deleting the entry,
or when the argument names an existing file but looks like an option (see the
ARGUMENTS section).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-completion \fIbash\fR|\fIzsh\fR|\fItcsh\fR
Parse the option and exit 0; this implementation prints
Please install shtab firstly! (upstream required the optional shtab
module to emit a script).
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-directory
Accepted for GNU rm compatibility and ignored: directories are always
trashed whole.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Silently ignore nonexistent files.
When both
\fB\-f\fR
and
\fB\-i\fR
are given, the last one wins.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Prompt, on standard output, before trashing each file; the prompt line ends
with a space:
.RS
.PP
.nf
trash\-put: trash <description> \(aq<path>\(aq?\ 
.fi
.RE
.IP
An answer whose first character is
\fBy\fR
or
\fBY\fR
confirms; anything else declines and leaves the file alone, which is not an
error.
<description>
is one of: regular file, regular empty file, directory, symbolic link, entry
(FIFO, socket or device node).
An end of file at the prompt is reported on standard error and the exit
status is 1.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-R ", " \-\-recursive
Accepted for GNU rm compatibility and ignored: directories are always
trashed recursively.
.TP
.B \-\-trash\-dir \fIDIR\fR
Use
.I DIR
as the trash can instead of the automatic choice (see TRASH DIRECTORIES
below).
.I DIR
is created if missing, with its
.IR files/ " and " info/
subdirectories in mode 0700.
The file to trash must be on the same volume as
.IR DIR ,
otherwise the attempt fails.
The value may also be attached,
.BI \-\-trash\-dir= DIR .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Report successes:
.RS
.PP
.nf
trash\-put: \(aq<path>\(aq trashed in <trash\-dir>
.fi
.RE
.IP
Repeat the option for more detail:
\fB\-vv\fR
also reports every trash directory as it is tried.
A leading
.I $HOME
in the trash directory is shortened to
\fB\(ti/\fR
in this message; set
.B TRASH_PUT_DISABLE_SHRINK
(=1) to disable the shortening.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the bare version number and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-force\-volume \fIVOLUME\fR
Pretend the file lives on
.IR VOLUME ,
skipping volume detection.
Mainly for testing and debugging.
.TP
.B \-\-home\-fallback
Add the home trash as a last\-resort candidate for files on other volumes.
Effective only when
.B TRASH_ENABLE_HOME_FALLBACK
is
.BR 1 .
Mainly for testing and debugging.
.SH ARGUMENTS
The files and directories to trash.
Trailing slashes are stripped from arguments
(\fIdir\fR/
trashes
\fIdir\fR);
a lone
\fB/\fR
survives.
Options may precede or follow the file list, but an option placed between
file arguments makes the trailing files unrecognized, which is a usage error
(exit status 2).
\fB\-\-\fR
ends option processing: later arguments are always treated as files.
An argument that starts with
\fB\-\fR
(a lone
\fB\-\fR
is exempt) and names an existing file is refused before anything is trashed:
.RS
.PP
.nf
trash\-put: error: refusing to treat existing file \-\-foo as an option (use \(aq./\-\-foo\(aq or a \(aq\-\-\(aq separator)
.fi
.RE
.PP
With no arguments at all:
.RS
.PP
.nf
usage: trash\-put [OPTION]... FILE...
trash\-put: error: Please specify the files to trash.
.fi
.RE
the exit status is 2;
\fB\-f\fR
does not bypass this.
.SH TRASH DIRECTORIES
A trash can is a directory with two subdirectories:
\fI<trash\-dir>/info/\fR
holding one
\fI<name>.trashinfo\fR
metadata file per trashed entry, and
\fI<trash\-dir>/files/\fR
holding the trashed content under the same stem.
When
.B trash\-put
creates the can,
.IR files/ " and " info/
are made with mode 0700.
.PP
Each
.I .trashinfo
file is created with mode 0600, before the entry is moved, and contains
exactly:
.RS
.PP
.nf
[Trash Info]
Path=<percent\-encoded original location>
DeletionDate=<YYYY\-MM\-DDTHH:MM:SS local time>
.fi
.RE
.PP
The original location is percent\-encoded per raw byte:
\fBA\-Z a\-z 0\-9 _ . \- \(ti /\fR
are kept literal, every other byte becomes
\fB%NN\fR;
a space becomes
\fB%20\fR,
a byte that is not valid UTF\-8 becomes
\fB%FF\fR,
and the round trip is byte\-exact.
Home\-trash entries store absolute paths; volume trash entries store paths
relative to their volume.
Name collisions in
.I files/
get the suffixes
\fB_1\fR
through
\fB_99\fR
and then a random suffix.
If the move into
.I files/
fails, the pair just written (backup copy and info file) is rolled back.
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-trash\-dir ,
the trash can for a file is picked from the following, in order, the first
that works:
.IP 1. 3
the home trash, when the file is on the same volume as the home trash;
.IP 2. 3
\fI$volume\fB/.Trash/\fI$uid\fR, which requires
\fB$volume\fB/.Trash\fR
to be an existing, non\-symlink, sticky directory;
.IP 3. 3
\fI$volume\fB/.Trash\-\fI$uid\fR.
.PP
With
\fB\-vv\fR
each attempt is reported on standard error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every argument was trashed, declined under
\fB\-i\fR,
or ignored under
\fB\-f\fR.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: no file arguments, unknown option, an option between file
arguments, or an existing file that looks like an option.
.TP
.B 74
At least one file could not be trashed.
.TP
.B 1
Fatal error, for example an end of file at an interactive prompt.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Location of the home trash,
\fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash\fR.
Used only when set and non\-empty; an empty value is treated as unset, per
the XDG basedir specification, so the home trash falls back to
\fI$HOME/.local/share/Trash\fR.
A relative value is used verbatim, resolved against the current directory.
.TP
.B HOME
Fallback location of the home trash,
\fI$HOME/.local/share/Trash\fR;
also the prefix shortened to
\fB\(ti/\fR
in
\fB\-v\fR
messages.
.TP
.B TRASH_ENABLE_HOME_FALLBACK
Must be
\fB1\fR
to make
\fB\-\-home\-fallback\fR
effective.
.TP
.B TRASH_PUT_DISABLE_SHRINK
When set to
.BR 1 , no
\fB\(ti/\fR
shortening is applied in
\fB\-v\fR
messages.
.TP
.B TRASH_PUT_FAKE_UID_FOR_TESTING
Integer overriding
.BR getuid (2)
when choosing
\fI$volume\fB/.Trash/\fI$uid\fR
and
\fI$volume\fB/.Trash\-\fI$uid\fR;
for testing.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash
The home trash, when
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is set and non\-empty.
.TP
.I $HOME/.local/share/Trash
The home trash otherwise.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.PP
.nf
trash\-put notes.txt
trash\-put \-\- \-dashfile
trash\-put \-\-trash\-dir=/tmp/trash \-v project/
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR trash (1),
.BR trash\-list (1),
.BR trash\-restore (1),
.BR trash\-empty (1),
.BR trash\-rm (1),
.BR rename (2).
.PP
Implements the FreeDesktop.org Trash Specification,
https://specifications.freedesktop.org/trash\-spec/
